WEBVTT

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:04.000
Identity is one of the most important design choices in an MCP system.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.000
A user may ask for an action, the AI host may decide how to assist,

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:12.000
an MCP server may expose the tool, and a downstream API may enforce permissions.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:15.000
Those layers should not be collapsed into one vague identity.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.000
User authorization is different from application authorization.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.955
A service account is different from a delegated user token.

00:00:20.955 --> 00:00:23.955
A model-generated request is different from verified user intent.

00:00:23.955 --> 00:00:28.987
Delegated access lets an application act on behalf of a user within defined limits.

00:00:28.987 --> 00:00:32.560
OAuth-style concepts such as scopes, audience, token lifetime,

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:36.000
and consent are useful mental models even when the exact implementation varies.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.955
Scope should describe what an integration can do.

00:00:38.955 --> 00:00:41.215
Audience should constrain where a token can be used.

00:00:41.215 --> 00:00:45.000
Consent should make the user or administrator aware of meaningful access.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.887
Token lifetime should limit exposure if credentials are mishandled.

00:00:47.887 --> 00:00:50.955
Careless token passthrough can blur trust boundaries.

00:00:50.955 --> 00:00:54.000
If a powerful user token is passed through multiple components, tools,

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:56.000
or servers without clear policy,

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:00.150
components may gain access they do not need and users may not understand what is being

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:00.955
authorized.

00:01:00.955 --> 00:01:04.000
Safer designs separate user intent from model-generated action requests,

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:08.000
issue narrowly scoped credentials where possible, keep secrets out of prompts and logs,

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:10.955
and enforce authorization at the application or service boundary.

00:01:10.955 --> 00:01:15.000
MCP tools should not gain permission merely because a model asks for them.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:19.000
The model can propose an action, but policy must decide whether the action is allowed.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:21.955
High-impact actions should require explicit consent or approval.

00:01:21.955 --> 00:01:25.000
Permission checks should consider the user, the tool, the resource,

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:29.215
the requested operation, the data sensitivity, and the current context.

00:01:29.215 --> 00:01:31.000
Consent is not a decorative prompt;

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:33.910
it is a control that helps connect user intent to authorized action.
